==English==

===Etymology===
According to Watkins, the first element is related to the animal {{m|en|cat}} and has correlations in {{cog|nl|kat||vessel with wide fore and aft}}, {{cog|ML.|gatus}}, {{m|la|gattus}}, {{cog|fro|chat}}, a war machine or portable shed for protecting sapping operations, or a boat with such a shed, and {{cog|frm|chat||kind of commercial vessel}}.<ref>{{R:American_Heritage_Dictionary|catboat}}</ref>

However, it could instead derive from {{der|en|ga|cot}}, {{m|ga|coit}}; see {{m|en|cot#Etymology_3|cot (sense 3)}}.<ref>{{R:MacBain|coit}}</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[sailing]] [[boat]] with a single sail, usually rigged on a [[gaff]] spar, used for [[fishing]] in [[New England]] and later adapted for [[racing]] and [[cruising]]. It has a single [[mast]] set near to the [[bow]] and a long [[boom]] which may extend over the [[stern]].
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=10|passage=Men that I knew around Wapatomac didn't wear high, shiny plug hats, nor yeller spring overcoats, nor carry canes with ivory heads as big as a '''catboat''''s anchor, as you might say.}}

